--TEST--
`& | ^ ~ << >>` agree on refusing every operand `rule:types/arithmetic`'s bitwise row has no entry for, `decimal` among them — `rule:types/conversion` and `rule:types/arithmetic`, `rule:types/arithmetic`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Every spelling in `rule:types/arithmetic`'s `& | ^ ~ << >>` row, over the operand
// domains that row has no entry for. What is asserted is that the six
// *agree* — one code and one sentence, whatever the operator and whatever
// the operand — rather than that any one of them reports. The accepted half
// of the same row is `the-bitwise-operators-answer-over-every-row.nvst`,
// which sweeps `int` and `uint` for the identities.
float $f = 1.5;
decimal $d = 1.5;
string $s = "ab";
bool $b = true;

// PHP converts first: `1.5 & 1.5` is `1`, `"ab" & "cd"` is bytewise, and
// `true & true` is `1`. `rule:types/conversion` has no implicit conversion for any of
// them to be. The `float` row is the one worth having a case for — before
// this refusal it answered `1.5`, a bit-and over the `f64`'s own bits, which
// is neither PHP's answer nor a wrong one that shows.
mixed $a1 = $f & $f;
mixed $a2 = $f | $f;
mixed $a3 = $f ^ $f;
mixed $a4 = $f << $f;
mixed $a5 = $f >> $f;
mixed $a6 = ~$f;

// A `decimal` parts from its own § 3 table here: `rule:types/arithmetic` grants
// arithmetic, equality and ordering, because a coefficient and a scale
// answer those exactly, and grants no bit operation because there is no bit
// pattern to read.
mixed $c1 = $d & $d;
mixed $c2 = ~$d;

// The two remaining domains.
mixed $e1 = $s & $s;
mixed $e2 = $b & $b;

// `-$f` is still arithmetic and still compiles: the line between the two
// refusals is that `rule:types/arithmetic` grants arithmetic over four numeric types
// and bit operations over two.
echo -$f, $a1, $a2, $a3, $a4, $a5, $a6, $c1, $c2, $e1, $e2;
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
